{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 23,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.072,
            0.159,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "108"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.11,
            0.881,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": "**Example 9:** The ratio of incomes of two persons is 9 : 7 and the ratio of their expenditures is 4 : 3. If each of them manages to save ₹2000 per month, find their monthly incomes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.168,
            0.883,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": "Since the ratio of the incomes of the two persons is 9 : 7, it means that for every 9 equal parts of income of the first person, the second person has 7 equal parts. If the equal part is x, the incomes of the two persons will be 9x and 7x."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.245,
            0.881,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "In a similar manner, we may consider their expenditures to be 4y and 3y, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.283,
            0.881,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": "Then the equations formed by subtracting the expenditure from the income leading to a saving of ₹ 2000 are as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.329,
            0.879,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "9x - 4y = 2000\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.383,
            0.351,
            0.879,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": "7x - 3y = 2000\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.379,
            0.879,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "To solve these by the method of elimination, we proceed as follows:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.404,
            0.882,
            0.503
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** We first decide which variable to eliminate. Suppose we wish to eliminate y from both equations. We multiply each side of Equation (1) by 3 [the coefficient of y in Equation (2)], and multiply Equation (2) by 4 [the coefficient of y in (1)]. By doing this the coefficients of y in both equations become equal. We get the equations:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.508,
            0.879,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "27x - 12y = 6000\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.366,
            0.533,
            0.879,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "28x - 12y = 8000\\tag{4}",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.564,
            0.881,
            0.624
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Subtract the LHS and RHS of Equation 3 from the LHS and RHS of Equation 4 respectively. Since we are subtracting equal quantities from equal quantities, we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.33,
            0.629,
            0.667,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": "(28x - 27x) - (12y - 12y) = 2000.",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.655,
            0.881,
            0.694
          ],
          "content": "This step eliminates the variable y, leaving us with (28x – 27x) = 2000 or x = 2000."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.699,
            0.67,
            0.72
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** Substituting this value of x in (1), we get"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.726,
            0.691,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": "9(2000)-4y=2000   i.e.,y=4000.",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.752,
            0.762,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": "Hence, the solution of the equations is x = 2000, y = 4000."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.777,
            0.881,
            0.815
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, the monthly incomes of the two persons are ₹18,000 and ₹14,000, respectively."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.815,
            0.881,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "Let us verify our solution. The ratio of the incomes is 18000 : 14000 = 9 : 7. Also, the ratio of their expenditures = (18000 – 2000) : (14000 – 2000) = 16000 : 12000 = 4 : 3. Hence, the conditions of the problem are satisfied."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
